A||||8||Kwic66||Lost My Fiero Key!||||
Z||000000||Kwic66||09-05-99||07:24 AM||||Hello Everyone!!,<br>  My wife has been packing some things to put in storage. She packed the cigar box that I had my FIERO key in.The car has been parked because of a blown head gasket.I'm now ready to fix it but we can't find the key!Is their any way I can have another key made without one to copy??I can get in the car.Can I take out the ignition switch and take it and have a key made for it??Any simple solutions would be greatly apreciated!!
Z||000001||GWMORGAN||09-05-99||07:42 AM||||Go to a GM dealer with the VIN for the car, they should be able to cut you a set of keys...good luck.
Z||000002||kel||09-05-99||01:42 PM||||Alot of dealerships will not do that anymore because of security reasons.  Anyone can get anyone else's VIN #.  If you happen to have the knock outs to the keys (I always keep them in a safe place) you can take them in and they will cut one for you, or if you have the invoice to your car, you can get the key codes off of that, and they'll cut a set.  If you bought your Fiero from a dealer, go to that dealership and ask.  When you have been there before, they would be more likely to cut you some.  Good Luck!   
Z||000003||theogre||09-05-99||05:26 PM||||kel is right. Take your title/registration and photo ID with you. That should likely quiet most of them that are worried..
Z||000004||Gokart||09-06-99||04:31 AM||||A locksmith will be sure to help. Probably wouldn't cost anymore than the dealer would charge. He will make one to get in, then one for the ignition.
Z||000005||Fiero64||09-06-99||07:45 AM||||Locksmiths generally charge an arm and a leg for situations like this.  I have a similar situation that I hope can be remedied by the dealercutting a set of keys, I just bought a Fiero from my father who has a small car lot and there are no keys for it, let me know if you have any luck with the dealer.....
Z||000006||Fiero64||09-08-1999||04:25 PM||||Just checked with my local Pontiac contact and he says that they can't go back before '91 for VIN key code info....Good luck getting that lock cylinder out without the keys...||||reg||
Z||000007||kel||09-09-1999||04:05 PM||||I just checked with my local Pontiac dealer.  He said that as long as you have the key codes off your invoice or the knock outs, they can cut you a set of keys.  No matter what the year.  Every GM dealership is provided with a book that lists that information.||||reg||
Z||000008||theogre||09-09-1999||04:59 PM||||The locks can be removed from both the doors/trunk and the column Without the keys. You can find locks in any scrap yard. they do not have to be from another fiero. practically any GM car will do for the column. You will need to dig around a bit to find the doors and trunk.<P>Pop-A-Lock has franchises in many areas, and is only like 25$ to get you into the car. once your in the rest is annoying and time consuming but not hard.<P>The door locks are removed from inside the door. the trunk lock is retained by a clip and a couple phillips screws. the steering column requires a wheel puller and lock plate tool. The lock itself is retained by a screw under the turn signal selector.||||reg||
